 A shared inference worker processes one request at a time. A burst can leave
 short requests waiting behind long requests. GateQueue combines a queue budget
 with shortest-estimated-service ordering. The contribution is an explicit
-admission decision before ordering work on the worker. We claim lower tail
-latency at equal service coverage.
+admission decision before ordering work on the worker. When the waiting queue
+contains eight requests, GateQueue rejects a new arrival and returns an overload
+status. Otherwise, it admits the request; when the worker becomes idle, it selects
+the queued request with the shortest estimated service time.
+
+In the synthetic burst trace in Table~\ref{tab:burst}, GateQueue lowers
+completed-request P99 latency from 100 ms to 80 ms relative to FIFO. GateQueue
+admits and completes 80 of 100 arrivals, while FIFO completes all 100; their
+completion rates are 8 and 10 requests/s, respectively. Rejected requests are
+excluded from latency quantiles. At low load, both policies complete all 100
+requests with P99 latency of 20 ms.
 
 The worker executes each admitted request to completion before selecting the
 next request. This non-preemptive policy avoids discarding partial execution.
